1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,770
At first, there is only a sound.

2
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:10,790
A person on a rooftop keeps crying out: ‘Ah.’

3
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:14,670
No explanation. No slogan. No demand.

4
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:22,270
The Howler on the Rooftop was presented at P4
Theater on January 2, 2021, as a one-time
performance.

5
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:27,110
In it, this unclaimed sound becomes the most
crowded thing in the room.

6
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:31,870
Liu Du searches for the source because it will
not let him sleep, work, or remain alone with
his own ambition.

7
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:35,490
When he finally reaches the rooftop, he does not
meet an answer.

8
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:39,163
He meets a person whose voice refuses to become
a sentence.

9
00:00:40,403 --> 00:00:42,553
That should leave the sound open.

10
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:45,493
Instead, everyone rushes to close it.

11
00:00:45,853 --> 00:00:48,653
A worker says the Howler cries for workers.

12
00:00:49,473 --> 00:00:55,153
A painter claims art. A musician claims music. A
writer claims literature. A lawyer claims the
law.

13
00:00:55,633 --> 00:01:00,173
The neighbor calls it a nuisance. The police
call it a disturbance. A reporter calls it a
headline.

14
00:01:00,693 --> 00:01:01,933
The voice has not changed.

15
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:03,453
The listeners have.

16
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:04,985
Each uses it as a mirror.

17
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:08,495
This is the trap at the center of the play.

18
00:01:08,595 --> 00:01:14,515
A sound without words appears available to
everyone, so everyone assumes it belongs to
them.

19
00:01:14,955 --> 00:01:17,075
They do not ask what the Howler needs.

20
00:01:17,715 --> 00:01:20,835
They give the howl a meaning that confirms their
own position.

21
00:01:21,555 --> 00:01:24,035
Listening becomes a way of speaking through
someone else.

22
00:01:24,595 --> 00:01:27,135
Then the city does what it knows how to do.

23
00:01:27,775 --> 00:01:32,475
It classifies the voice, publicizes it, worships
it, and tries to silence it.

24
00:01:32,515 --> 00:01:37,395
What it cannot do is remain beside the voice
without turning it into something useful.

25
00:01:38,215 --> 00:01:40,837
Even sympathy can become another way of taking
possession.

26
00:01:42,077 --> 00:01:45,447
The production process makes this harder, not
easier.

27
00:01:46,507 --> 00:01:52,427
P4U brought strangers together through a short
rehearsal process, while the 3P group recorded
interviews and reactions around the work.

28
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:54,627
The surviving accounts do not agree.

29
00:01:55,047 --> 00:01:58,007
Some participants loved the freedom and the
people they met.

30
00:01:58,327 --> 00:02:02,787
Others described confusion, insecurity,
pressure, or the wish to leave.

31
00:02:03,287 --> 00:02:06,247
One participant called the project more an
action than a play.

32
00:02:06,707 --> 00:02:12,757
Another asked whether repeated demands for
improvisation really gave people with different
experience an equal chance to speak.

33
00:02:13,997 --> 00:02:17,147
That disagreement is not a flaw to edit out.

34
00:02:17,667 --> 00:02:19,347
It is the second howl.

35
00:02:20,027 --> 00:02:22,947
The stage asks what happens to a voice with no
language.

36
00:02:23,627 --> 00:02:30,107
The archive asks what happens to a voice that
has language, has been recorded, and still may
not change the next decision.

37
00:02:30,627 --> 00:02:34,098
A microphone can preserve dissent. It cannot, by
itself, answer it.

38
00:02:35,338 --> 00:02:39,808
By the end, the howl is no longer securely
attached to one body.

39
00:02:40,368 --> 00:02:41,648
It passes through the company.

40
00:02:42,608 --> 00:02:43,948
Violence does not settle its meaning.

41
00:02:44,728 --> 00:02:46,988
The sound remains because the problem remains.

42
00:02:47,608 --> 00:02:50,528
The real question is not: What does the Howler
represent?

43
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:55,674
Can a community let a voice remain someone
else’s before turning it into everyone’s symbol?

44
00:02:56,915 --> 00:02:58,765
A voice needs more than visibility.

45
00:02:59,345 --> 00:03:02,165
It needs a listener who can resist speaking for
it.

46
00:03:02,205 --> 00:03:10,365
If listening is real, something after the
listening must be allowed to change: a
rehearsal, a rule, a relationship, or the right
to leave.

47
00:03:10,925 --> 00:03:12,745
Otherwise the voice is not heard.

48
00:03:13,185 --> 00:03:14,219
It is only used.

